{
  "$comment": "Impact surfaces found by pattern in an area's source files. `patterns` and `exclude` are case-insensitive regexes run per line on the code with comments and string literals blanked, so a provider named in a CSP header, marketing copy, an API description or a comment doesn't count; `raw_patterns` run on the line as written, so SQL inside a string (op.execute(\"ALTER TABLE x DROP COLUMN y\"), an f-string TRUNCATE) and directives count; the bare TRUNCATE and DROP forms are upper-case only, so prose doesn't match. `path_patterns` match the file's path (a page or screen module of a UI). Decorated Python handlers (Flask, FastAPI, Azure Functions triggers) are entry points; calls through an API client wrapper (apiClient.postJson) are external integrations. (?-i:...) makes part of a pattern case-sensitive, which is how a camelCase verb (captureMessage) is told from capture( or capture_payment(. A line that matches one of the class's exclude patterns doesn't count. An optional `langs` restricts a class to files with those suffixes (the systems/CLI pack is Rust-only), and an optional `lang_exclude` maps a suffix to extra excludes that apply only to files of that language (so a Rust `drop(` or `matcher: &M` does not fire the web-tuned classes without weakening JS/TS detection). Type declaration files (.d.ts) are skipped. The highest-scoring class an area matches sets its surface factor; a person may judge it differently, with a reason. Patterns are about doing (charging, deleting, authenticating, enforcing access), not mentioning or reading: a catalogue that shows a price does not move money, and useSession() reads who is signed in without enforcing anything. NOTE: the money/access/personal-data classes are tuned for web and app stacks; outside them (systems and CLI code) a surface factor that rests only on these patterns is low-trust and should be flagged, not presented as confidently assessed. Call-aware patterns don't match a TypeScript member signature with no body (`settle(): Promise<void>;` in an interface or abstract class): it declares, it doesn't do. Bare capture/settle/transfer need a money noun (capturePayment, settle_invoice) or a money argument on the line, because in UI-automation code they mean 'take a screenshot' or 'wait until stable'; truncate needs a table or data noun, since truncating a string destroys nothing; Node stream and socket .destroy() and optional-chained map deletes (seen?.delete(k)) are not data destruction. The `secrets` class is credential handling (redaction, an Authorization header set, basic auth, storing or rotating a secret). The `public_api` class (`manifest_entries`) also scores the files a package manifest names as its public entry points: package.json main, module, exports and bin (a dist/, lib/ or build/ target is mapped back to its src/ file) and pyproject [project.scripts]; for a library or CLI that is what every user calls.",
  "classes": {
    "money": {
